data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR-Cas9 uses a guide RNA to locate target DNA, and the Cas9 protein cuts the DNA at that spot [1].\n- It is based on a bacterial antiviral defense system: Cas9 paired with a synthetic guide RNA cuts the genome at a chosen location, allowing genes to be removed or added [7].\n- Scientists commonly use it to \"knock out\" genes by breaking them. It can also supply a DNA repair template to replace a disease-causing mutation with a healthy sequence [3].\n- One study applies CRISPR SWAPnDROP to genome editing and large-scale DNA transfer between bacterial species, using *Escherichia coli* as a model organism [4]."}

